\tab b. MPLAB X setup and UPDI programmers.\par
\tab Download and install MPLAB X IDE from the Microchip website.[3]\par
\tab Open MPLAB and connect the SNAP module to the computer.\par
\tab Select File > New Project Microchip Embedded > Standalone Project then Next >.\par
\tab In the Select Device pop-up, use \par
\tab\tab Family: 8-bit AVR MCUs (XMega/Mega/Tiny/AVR)\par
\tab\tab Device: ATtiny412\par
\tab\tab Tool: No Tool. The UPDI programmer will be added later.\par
\tab\tab Next >\par
\tab In the Select Compiler, pick XC8. Next >\par
\tab Select a Project Name and Folder to store the project into. Finish.\par
\tab Once the project is set up, RMC Source File in the Projects list then New > avr-main.c\par
\tab Change File Name to main. Finish.\par
\par
\tab c. Blink the LED.\par
\tab Replace the main()\{ \} code in the main.c file with this code.\par
\f1\tab int main(void)\par
\tab\{\par

\pard\sl240\slmult1\tx1080\tx1440\tx1800\tx2160\tx2520\tab uint16_t count = 0;\par
\tab /* set PA7 of PORT A as an output pin */\par
\tab PORTA.DIR |= PIN7_bm;\par
\par
\tab // Toggle pin every 65536 counts.\par
\tab while (1) \{\par
\tab\tab if( count == 0) \{\par
\tab\tab\tab PORTA_OUTTGL = PIN7_bm;\par
\tab\tab\}\par
\tab\tab ++count;\par
\tab\}\par

\pard\sl240\slmult1\tab\}\f0\par
\par
\tab d. Compile the code and download it into the device.\par
\par
SECTION II - Exploration of Basic Hardware\par
3. Digital Input/Output (I/O)\par
\tab Now for an explanation of what it took to control the LED.\par
\tab A typical red LED requires about 1ma of current flowing thourgh it to generate detectable light. Almost all \par
\tab of them are rated for a maximum of 20ma continous current and will tollerate short pulses of 40ma or more.\par
\tab A little electronics theory will explain this common LED circuit seen in many examples. It consists of a \par
\tab resistor in series with an LED and is designed to result in a nominal 5ma to 10ma to light up the LED. The \par
\tab resistor has to be adjusted based on the power source.\par
\tab Two circuits are shown; one using a 5v source (typical of many microcontrollers) and another using a 3.7v\par
\tab source (a system using a LiPo battery as its power source). A typical RED LED has a voltage drop of about \par
\tab 1.7v. The resistor has to cause a voltage drop such that the total of voltage changes in the circuit is zero.\par
\tab Using OHM's law, E = I*R, or E/R = I to calculate the resistor value for a nominal current value, we have.\par
\par
\tab +5 --------/\\/\\/\\-------------|>|-------GND\tab +3.7 --------/\\/\\/\\-------------|>|-------GND\par

\pard\sl240\slmult1\tx1710\tx3150\tx6120\tx7650\tab 470\tab D\tab 270\tab D\par

\pard\sl240\slmult1\tx1080\tx3060\tx5580\tx7560\tab 3.3v/470 = 7.0ma\tab 1.7v\tab 2.0v/270 = 7.4ma\tab 1.7v\par

\pard\sl240\slmult1\par
\tab So, +5 - 3.3v - 1.7v = 0 and +3.7 - 2.0v - 1.7v = 0.\par
\par
\tab a. Pick a pin. Configure the PORT.\par
\tab Now that the external circuit parameters are known, 5v @ 7.0ma or 3.7v @ 7.4ma, to light the LED, you \par
\tab need to determine if this microcontroller can meet these requirements.\par
\tab We will use the ATtiny412 device and a 3.6 AAA battery pack with NiCad rechargable batteries and PA7 \par
\tab as the control pin.\par
\tab Section 35.10 Table 35-16 states the I/O Pin Characteristics.\par
\tab V\sub OH\nosupersub  gives the I/O pin drive strength with V\sub DD\nosupersub  = 3.0v and an output current of 7.5ma, the pin will \par
\tab maintain a minimum level of 2.4v. So for a V\sub DD\nosupersub  of 3.6v, it should maintain about 3.2v or so which is \par
\tab still high enough to light the LED.\par
\tab Note that higher currents are available with higher V\sub DD\nosupersub  supply voltages. i.e. use +5v V\sub DD\nosupersub  and a smaller \par
\tab resistor (allowing more current to flow) for brighter LEDs!\par
\tab b. Section 16.3.2.2 states that the Virtual PORT resgister map method should be used to control \par
\tab I/O port. This provides some benifits, as explained, but we will use the standard name PORT.\par
\tab Table 16-1 show that to access the regular port registers, just use VPORT instead of PORT.\par
\par
\tab c. Output Options.\par
\tab\tab A. Source and Sink current considerations.\par
\tab\tab The example schematic in Figure 1 is using the MCU pin to source current to the LED. The \par
\tab\tab current flows from out of the pin, though the LED and resistor, and into ground (the negative \par
\tab\tab side of the battery).\par
\tab\tab The LED could have been connected to VCC and then the MCU pin would act a a current sink. \par
\tab\tab The current would flow from the battery plus terminal, through the LED and resistor, and into \par
\tab\tab the MCU pin.\par
\tab\tab Whether the circuit is configured for a source or a sink, the calculations for the resistor still \par
\tab\tab have to result in the total voltage changes being zero and the MCU pin has to be able to \par
\tab\tab handle the source current or the sink current. Alway check the data sheet! Some MCUs can \par
\tab\tab sink more current than they can source.\par
\tab\tab B. Control Logic\par
\tab\tab To use an I/O PORT pin as an output, source or sink, the bit in the direction register \par
\tab\tab PORTA.DIR associated to the pin must be set to a '1'. The example code line\par

\pard\sl240\slmult1\tx1080\tx1440\tx1800\tx2160\tx2520\f1\tab\tab\tab PORTA.DIR |= PIN7_bm;\par

\pard\sl240\slmult1\f0\tab\tab does this. The 'x' in PORTx has been replaced with the port being used; an 'A' for this \par
\tab\tab device. Some devices have many ports: PORTA, PORTB, PORTC, etc. Just use the approprate \par
\tab\tab letter for the port to be controlled.\par
\tab\tab The \f1 PIN7_bm\f0  is a defined litteral for the bit pattern \f1 1000 0000\f0 . Looking at 16.5.1[2], the \par
\tab\tab DIR register, this pattern OR'd into the register will 'set' bit 7 to a '1' and cause PA7 to be an \par
\tab\tab output pin with its output driver enabled.\par
\tab\tab The output pin can be set HIGH or LOW by writing a '1' or a '0' respectively using the \par
\tab\tab\tab\f1 PORTA.OUT = <pattern>\f0  \par
\tab\tab command. It the example code to toggle the bit, the\par
\tab\tab\tab\f1 PORTA_OUTTGL = PIN7_bm;\f0  \par
\tab\tab command is used with the bit pattern for pin 7.\par
\tab\tab Other control registers are discussed in this section and you may want to experiment with them.\par
\tab\tab\par
\tab d. Input Options.\par
\tab\tab A. Pull-ups considerations.\par
\tab\tab The example schematic in Figure 2 has added a push-button switch to circuit from Figure 1.\par
\tab\tab This is a common configuration use for simple user inputs. Note that is does not have a \par
\tab\tab external pull-up resistor to pull the line HIGH when the button is not pressed. To minimize \par
\tab\tab part count, most MCUs have optional internal pull-up resistors. Section 36.10[2] shows this \par
\tab\tab internal resistor to be about 35k, when activated.\par
\par

