\pard\sl240\slmult1\tab\tab To use an I/O PORT pin as an input, the bit in the direction register \par
\tab\tab PORTA.DIR associated to the pin must be set to a '0'. Since this is the reset condition of the bit \par
\tab\tab (see section 16.5.1[2], nothing needs to be done. If you need to clear this bit due to other \par
\tab\tab code operations, this example line of code could be used.\par

\pard\sl240\slmult1\tx1080\tx1440\tx1800\tx2160\tx2520\f1\tab\tab\tab\tab PORTA.DIR &= ~PIN1_bm;\par

\pard\sl240\slmult1\f0\tab\tab It inverts the bit mask \f1 0000 0010\f0  to \f1 1111 1101\f0  and AND's it to the DIR register contents.\par
\tab\tab Another register, PORTA.PINnCTRL (see 16.5.11 [2]) also affects the operation of this pin. The \par
\tab\tab 'n' refers to the pin number, so 'n=1'  in this case.\par
\tab\tab The reset (default) settings for all pins is Non-Inverted, Pull-up disabled, and INTDISABLE.\par
\tab\tab Since the pull-up for pin 1 will be used set bit 3 to '1' with\par

\pard\sl240\slmult1\tx1080\tx1440\tx1800\tx2160\tx2520\f1\tab\tab\tab\tab PORTA.PIN11CTRL |= (1<<3);\tab // set PULLUPEN = 1\par

\pard\sl240\slmult1\f0\tab\tab Read the state of the pins into a variable. For example, use\par
\tab\tab\tab\f1 portA_pins = PORTA.IN;\tab // read all the pins of PORTA.\f0\par
\tab\tab To test this new configuration, replace the main(void) code (main02.c) with the following:\par
\f1\par
\fs20     int main(void) \{\par
        uint8_t portA_pins = 0;\par
\par
        /* set PA7 of PORT A as an OUTPUT pin. The other bits are left as '0'\par
         * so that their associated pins will be INPUT pins. */\par
        PORTA.DIR |= PIN7_bm;\par
    \par
        /* enable the internal Pull-up resistor for PORTA pin 1 */\par
        PORTA.PIN1CTRL |= (1<<3);   // shift the '1' into the bit3 position.\par
\par
        // Control the LED with the push button switch.\par
        while (1) \{\par
            portA_pins = PORTA.IN;          // read all the pins of PORTA.\par
            portA_pins &= PIN1_bm;          // set all bits except bit.1 to '0'.\par
            if( portA_pins == 0) \{\par
                /* turn ON the LED if bit.1 (pin1) is LOW (i.e. switch is closed) */\par
                PORTA_OUTSET = PIN7_bm;\par
            \} else \{\par
                /* turn OFF the LED if pin1 is HIGH (i.e. switch is open) */\par
                PORTA_OUTCLR = PIN7_bm;\par
            \}\par
        \}\par
    \}\f0\par
\fs22\par
\tab\tab B. Compile the code and download it into the device. The LED can now be controlled by the \par
`\tab\tab switch.\par
\par
\tab Now that basic INPUT and OUTPUT control is available, try adding more LEDs and switches to do \par
\tab more complex operations. A simple system might use two LEDs, one RED and one GREEN, and three \par
\tab switches; A, B, and C. When a majority of switches are pressed the GREEN LED is turned ON. If not, \par
\tab the RED LED is lit instead.\par
\par
4. Timing - Part 1 - Simple Timer - TCA\par
ref: {{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK https://ww1.microchip.com/downloads/aemDocuments/documents/MCU08/ApplicationNotes/ApplicationNotes/TB3217-Getting-Started-with-TCA-DS90003217.pdf }}{\fldrslt{https://ww1.microchip.com/downloads/aemDocuments/documents/MCU08/ApplicationNotes/ApplicationNotes/TB3217-Getting-Started-with-TCA-DS90003217.pdf\ul0\cf0}}}}\f0\fs22\par
\tab Section 10[2] describes the clock control registers. On reset, these registers default to generating a \par
\tab 3.3 MHz periferal clock (CLK_PER) that is used as the base clock for all periferals. This will be the \par
\tab starting point for exploring Timer A; a 16-bit Timer/Counter described in Section 20[2]. The goal is to \par
\tab blink the LED in Figure 1 at a constant rate.\par
\tab a. Configuring the TCA to count up and set a bit on overflow.\par
\tab The TCA has many features and operating mode. The ref above explores them and provides example code \par
\tab to experiment with. This section will look at the most basic of timer operations: counting internal clock \par
\tab pulses (CLK_PER).\par
\tab The counter will be used in the NORMAL mode (see 20.3.3.1[2]). This is a 16-bit counter and will \par
\tab overflow back to 0 every 65356 counts. The CLK_PER is 3.3 MHz (3.3 x 10\super 6\nosupersub ) which would cause the \par
\tab the counter to overflow every 3.3x10\super 6\nosupersub  MHz/ 65356 = 50.5 Hz. Too fast to see a blinking LED. Using the \par
\tab TCA.CTRLA to divide the incoming CLK_PER by 64 will slow the overflow rate down to about 1.3 seconds. \par
\tab The counter also must be enabled to operate. The code below can be used to do these tasks.\par
\f1\par
\tab /* set Normal mode */\par
\tab TCA0.SINGLE.CTRLB = TCA_SINGLE_WGMODE_NORMAL_gc;\par
\par
\tab TCA0.SINGLE.CTRLA = TCA_SINGLE_CLKSEL_DIV64_gc  /* set CLK_PER/64) */\par
\tab\tab\tab\tab\tab | TCA_SINGLE_ENABLE_bm; \tab /* start timer */\f0\par
\par
\tab When it overflows, it will set the \par
\tab OVF bit is the INTFLAGS register (see 20.5.11[2]). This bit is not cleared automatically and must be \par
\tab cleared by writting a '1' to its bit location to allow it to bet set on the next overflow. The following \par
\tab example code can be used to do this.\par
\f1\par
\tab /* The OVF flag has to be cleared manually */\par
\tab TCA0.SINGLE.INTFLAGS = TCA_SINGLE_OVF_bm;\f0\par
\par
\tab The following example code (main03.c) will use this information to toggle the LED in Figure 1 on or off \par
\tab approximatly every 1.3 seconds.\par
\par
\f1 int main(void) \{\par
    /* set PA7 of PORT A as an OUTPUT pin. The other bits are left as '0'\par
     * so that their associated pins will be INPUT pins. */\par
    PORTA.DIR |= PIN7_bm;\par
\par
    /* *** TCA Configuration as NORMAL counter *** */\par
    /* set Normal mode */\par
    TCA0.SINGLE.CTRLB = TCA_SINGLE_WGMODE_NORMAL_gc;\par
\par
    TCA0.SINGLE.CTRLA = TCA_SINGLE_CLKSEL_DIV64_gc  /* set CLK_PER/64) */\par
        \tab               | TCA_SINGLE_ENABLE_bm;     /* start timer */\par
\par
    // Blink the LED at a rate set by the TCA overflow rate.\par
    while (1) \{\par
        /* Test for overflow */\par
        if( TCA0.SINGLE.INTFLAGS & TCA_SINGLE_OVF_bm) \{\par
            /* reset the flag */\par
            /* The OVF flag has to be cleared manually */\par
            TCA0.SINGLE.INTFLAGS = TCA_SINGLE_OVF_bm;\par
            /* and toggle the LED state. */\par
            PORTA_OUTTGL = PIN7_bm;\par
        \}\par
    \}\par
\}\f0\par
\par
\tab Compile the code and download it into the device. The LED is now being controlled by TCA timer.\par
\par
5. Timing - Part 2 - Simple Timer - TCA using its interrupt\par
\tab Configuring the TCA for NORMAL operation is the same as in the previous section. The additional \par
\tab step is to enable the interrupt on overflow bit in register \f1 TCA0.INTCTRL\f0 . The following code \par
\tab can do that.\par
\par
\f1\tab /* enable overflow interrupt */\par
\tab TCA0.SINGLE.INTCTRL = TCA_SINGLE_OVF_bm;\f0\par
\par
\tab SInce an interrupt is being active, it must have a interrupt handler (ISR) to take control when the MCU \tab jumps to the interrupts vector. The following example code will do that. This code wil also toggle the \par
\tab LED.\par
\par
\f1\tab ISR(TCA0_OVF_vect)\par
\tab\{\par
\tab     /* toggle the LED state. */\par
\tab     PORTA_OUTTGL = PIN7_bm;\par
\tab     /* The interrupt flag has to be cleared manually */\par
\tab     TCA0.SINGLE.INTFLAGS = TCA_SINGLE_OVF_bm;\par
\tab\}\par
\f0\par
\tab Additionally, the interrupt.h header is needed and the command to enable global interrupts. This is \par
\tab the modified main.c file (main04.c) code for this example. The changes are in \b BOLD\b0 .\par
\par
\f1 #include <avr/io.h>\par
\b #include <avr/interrupt.h>          // to support the use of interrupts\par
\b0\par
\b /* TCA interrupt service routine. */\par
ISR(TCA0_OVF_vect)\par
\{\par
    /* toggle the LED state. */\par
    PORTA_OUTTGL = PIN7_bm;\par
    /* The interrupt flag has to be cleared manually */\par
    TCA0.SINGLE.INTFLAGS = TCA_SINGLE_OVF_bm;\par
\}\par
\b0\par
int main(void) \{\par

\pard\sl240\slmult1\tx450\tab /* set PA7 of PORT A as an OUTPUT pin. The other bits are left as '0'\par
\tab * so that their associated pins will be INPUT pins. */\par
\tab PORTA.DIR |= PIN7_bm;\par
\par
\b\tab /* *** TCA Configuration as NORMAL counter with interrupt *** */\par
\tab /* enable overflow interrupt */\par
\tab TCA0.SINGLE.INTCTRL = TCA_SINGLE_OVF_bm;\par
\b0\par
\tab /* set Normal mode */\par
\tab TCA0.SINGLE.CTRLB = TCA_SINGLE_WGMODE_NORMAL_gc;\par
\par
\tab TCA0.SINGLE.CTRLA = TCA_SINGLE_CLKSEL_DIV64_gc  /* set CLK_PER/64) */\par
\tab\tab\tab\tab\tab | TCA_SINGLE_ENABLE_bm; \tab /* start timer */\par
\par
\b\tab /* enable Global interrupts */\par
\tab sei();\par
\b0     \par
\tab // Blink the LED at a rate set by the TCA overflow rate.\par
\tab while (1) \{\par
\b\tab     ;       // LED control is handled by the interrupt service.\par
\b0\tab\}\par

\pard\sl240\slmult1\}\par
\f0\par
\tab Compile the code and download it into the device. The LED is now being controlled by TCA timer \par
\tab interrrupt service routine.\par
\par
6. Timing - Part 3 - System Timer\par
\tab A very common resource in many systems is the one millisecond time reference or millis() function. \par
This function returns an value equal to the number of milliseconds that the system has been operating \par
since reset. The value is normally an unsigned long or uint32_t and will overflow about every \tab 50 days so, \par
long enough for most projects.\par
TCA will be configured to generate an interrupt every 1ms and additional code will be added to provide the \par
millis() function. The millis() function will then be used to control the LED in Figure 1.\par
\par
\tab The first task is to configure the TCA to interrupt every 1ms. Since the CLK_PER is at 3,333,333 Hz and \par
if the prescaler is set to x1, the counter will be at 1ms after 3,333 counts. The TCA0_SINGLE_PER register sets \par
the TOP value for the counter. This is the overflow point. At reset, this TOP value is 0xFFFF, so the full 16-bits \par
of the counter are used. The configuration code for the TCA is now:\par
\par

\pard\sl240\slmult1\tx450\f1\tab /* *** TCA Configuration as NORMAL counter with interrupt *** */\par
\tab TCA0_SINGLE_PER = 0x0D04;\tab // 3,333 - 1 since the counter starts at 0.\par
\tab TCA0.SINGLE.INTCTRL = TCA_SINGLE_OVF_bm;\par
\tab TCA0.SINGLE.CTRLB = TCA_SINGLE_WGMODE_NORMAL_gc;\par
\tab TCA0.SINGLE.CTRLA = TCA_SINGLE_CLKSEL_DIV1_gc  /* set CLK_PER/1) */\par
\tab\tab\tab\tab\tab | TCA_SINGLE_ENABLE_bm; \tab   /* start timer */\par

\pard\sl240\slmult1\f0\par
The ISR will now update a global variable to accumulate 1m 'ticks'. A service function will allow other parts of \par
the program to access the accumulated value in a reliable method. This is the additional code.\par
\par
\f1 volatile static uint32_t totalMilliseconds;\tab // global variable\par
\par
/* This interrupt service is called every 1 ms. */\par
ISR(TCA0_OVF_vect)\par
\{\par
\tab ++totalMilliseconds;\par
\tab TCA0.SINGLE.INTFLAGS = TCA_SINGLE_OVF_bm;\tab // clear interrupt flag.\par
\}\par
\par
/* Return the total number of milliseconds since the project started. */\par
uint32_t millis()\par
\{\par
\tab uint32_t temp;\tab\tab\tab // make a holder for the counter.\par
\par
\tab cli();\tab\tab\tab\tab // Turn OFF interrupts to avoid corruption\par
\tab\tab\tab\tab\tab\tab // during a multi-byte read.\par
\tab temp = totalMilliseconds;\tab // get a copy while interrupts are disabled.\par
\tab sei();\tab\tab\tab\tab // Turn interrupts back ON.\par
\par
\tab return temp;\tab\tab\tab // return a 'clean' copy of the counter.\par
\}\f0\par
\par
The new main.c file (main05.c) should now look like this.\par
\par
\f1 #include <avr/io.h>\par
#include <avr/interrupt.h>      // to support the use of interrupts\par
\par
#define LED_DELAY\tab 1000UL\tab // N * 1ms\par
\par
volatile static uint32_t totalMilliseconds;\tab // global variable\par
\par
uint32_t millis();              // prototype for function\par
\par
/* TCA interrupt service routine. */\par
ISR(TCA0_OVF_vect)\par
\{\par
   ++totalMilliseconds;\par
   TCA0.SINGLE.INTFLAGS = TCA_SINGLE_OVF_bm;   // clear the interrupt flag\par
\}\par
\par
/* Return the total number of milliseconds since the project started. */\par
uint32_t millis()\par
\{\par

\pard\sl240\slmult1\tx450\tab uint32_t temp;\tab\tab\tab // make a holder for the counter.\par
\par
\tab cli();\tab\tab\tab\tab // Turn OFF interrupts to avoid corruption\par
\tab\tab\tab\tab\tab\tab // during a multi-byte read.\par
\tab temp = totalMilliseconds;\tab // get a copy while interrupts are disabled.\par
\tab sei();\tab\tab\tab\tab // Turn interrupts back ON.\par
\par
\tab return temp;\tab\tab\tab // return a 'clean' copy of the counter.\par

\pard\sl240\slmult1\}\par
\par
int main(void) \{\par

\pard\sl240\slmult1\tx450\tx900\tx1350    \tab uint32_t ledTime = 0UL;\par
\par
\tab /* set PA7 of PORT A as an OUTPUT pin. The other bits are left as '0'\par
     * so that their associated pins will be INPUT pins. */\par
\tab PORTA.DIR |= PIN7_bm;\par
\par
    /* *** TCA Configuration as NORMAL counter with interrupt for 1ms OVF *** */\par
\tab TCA0_SINGLE_PER = 0x0D04;    // 3,333 - 1 since the counter starts at 0.\par
   \tab /* enable overflow interrupt */\par
\tab TCA0.SINGLE.INTCTRL = TCA_SINGLE_OVF_bm;\par
\tab /* set Normal mode */\par
\tab TCA0.SINGLE.CTRLB = TCA_SINGLE_WGMODE_NORMAL_gc;\par
\tab TCA0.SINGLE.CTRLA = TCA_SINGLE_CLKSEL_DIV1_gc  /* set CLK_PER/64) */\par
\tab\tab\tab\tab\tab | TCA_SINGLE_ENABLE_bm; \tab /* start timer */\par
\par
    /* enable Global interrupts */\par
    sei();\par
   \par
    // Blink the LED at a rate set by the TCA overflow rate.\par
    while (1) \{\par
\tab\tab if( millis() > ledTime ) \{\par
\tab\tab\tab ledTime = millis() + LED_DELAY;\par
          /* toggle the LED state. */\par
          PORTA_OUTTGL = PIN7_bm;\par
\tab\tab\}\par
   \}\par

\pard\sl240\slmult1\}\f0\par
\par
Compile the code and download it into the device. The LED is now being controlled by timing test in the \par
while() loop using the millis() function based on the 1ms interrupt 'tick' from the TCA interrrupt service \par
routine.\par
\par
7. General Programming\par
With the basics of Input, Output, and Timing covered, the support code for these functions should be organized \par
into their own files for better version and configuration control. This will be very helpful as the project grows \par
and coding becomes more complex.\par
A common architecture may look like this:\par
\tab a. main.c\tab - Overall project control.\par
\tab b. systime.c\tab - time support and timer initialization functions.\par
\tab      systime.h\tab - header file to be added to main.c to access time functions.\tab\par
\tab c. io_ctrl.c\tab - input/output control and initialization functions.\par
\tab      io_ctrl.h\tab - header file to be added to main.c to access i/o functions.\par
\par
The current project, reorgainized in this method, can be downloaded from " ".\par
This is one of many way to organize your projects.\par
